1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:03,100
Most games give you a fixed set of rules.

2
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:07,500
Patch Notes Live lets you change them while
you're playing.

3
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:12,380
I'm Aditya, and this is a survival game where
your next idea becomes actual

4
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:13,220
game code.

5
00:00:13,925 --> 00:00:16,345
Let's make enemies run away from the player.

6
00:00:16,405 --> 00:00:19,385
I type one sentence into the rule box.

7
00:00:19,445 --> 00:00:23,425
Autopilot takes over while I'm typing, so the
game keeps moving.

8
00:00:23,445 --> 00:00:28,425
This local run uses live Codex generation, not an
archive match.

9
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:35,820
Codex opens a fresh temporary workspace and
writes one JavaScript mod.

10
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:39,180
The panel shows editing and testing progress.

11
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:41,580
I've shortened the wait here.

12
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:47,155
The server then runs its own verification instead
of trusting the model's

13
00:00:47,175 --> 00:00:49,835
claim that the tests passed.

14
00:00:50,295 --> 00:00:56,355
Seven checks cover loading, stability,
instruction budget, runaway spawning,

15
00:00:56,375 --> 00:00:59,930
survival, visible effect, and determinism.

16
00:00:59,950 --> 00:01:02,690
The simulator tries three seeds.

17
00:01:02,750 --> 00:01:07,970
Each approved mod runs inside its own isolated
Quick JavaScript sandbox

18
00:01:08,190 --> 00:01:12,930
with memory and instruction limits and no network
or browser access.

19
00:01:13,725 --> 00:01:16,185
Now the patch is live.

20
00:01:16,205 --> 00:01:21,385
The version changes, the rule appears in the
patch notes, and enemies start

21
00:01:21,405 --> 00:01:23,545
fleeing without reloading the game.

22
00:01:23,565 --> 00:01:26,225
This isn't a menu of predefined power ups.

23
00:01:26,430 --> 00:01:29,970
Codex wrote the behavior for the sentence we just
entered.

24
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:33,010
Up to three patches can run together.

25
00:01:33,070 --> 00:01:35,970
Add a fourth, and the oldest expires.

26
00:01:36,110 --> 00:01:41,715
A watchdog also reverts mods that repeatedly
fault or become too slow.

27
00:01:41,735 --> 00:01:46,835
Verification tests a limited window, so this live
backstop matters after

28
00:01:46,855 --> 00:01:48,315
a patch has passed.

29
00:01:48,615 --> 00:01:53,195
The public browser demo works without a server or
an API key.

30
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:59,070
It honestly labels its twenty-five archived
patches so judges can try huge

31
00:01:59,090 --> 00:02:03,950
players, rainbow enemies, or shrinking walls
immediately.

32
00:02:03,970 --> 00:02:08,870
New AI generated rules need the local setup and
Codex access.

33
00:02:09,675 --> 00:02:16,855
The engine, sandbox, verifier, and live patch
pipeline share one TypeScript

34
00:02:16,875 --> 00:02:17,735
project.

35
00:02:17,755 --> 00:02:22,405
The code is public and the browser demo is ready
to try.

36
00:02:22,425 --> 00:02:24,005
Type a rule.

37
00:02:24,025 --> 00:02:29,725
Watch the game rewrite itself, and please leave
the enemies some dignity.
